  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1229 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nTuesday through Saturday:\n\nAs we remain stuck in this pattern with low pressure over Hudson\nBay and high pressure over the southern U.S. with weak shortwaves\nin between, the forecast for southern WI will continue to be\nuncertain in terms of storm chances and timing. Right now, it\nlooks like the main 850mb frontal boundary will end up stationed\nin northern IL rather than southern WI, but there is still room\nfor that boundary to wiggle northward at times. That frontal\nboundary will be the focus for storm generation from Tuesday\nthrough Thursday morning. \n\nSo Monday night/Tuesday morning storms will once again depend on\nthe Monday storm movement and where the outflow and effective\nfront land. It will also depend on where the low level jet\ndevelops Mon nt (and the position of the nose of the LLJ). It will\nalso depend on if there is a shortwave tracking across that\nlocation at the same time in order to help initiate more organized\nconvection. Therefore, we have highest chances (55-65%) for storms in\nthe southern tier of counties Tuesday morning and lower chances\n(30-45%) in the afternoon. The afternoon scenario would be for\nadditional storms firing along the stalled outflow boundary\n(again, depends on where the morning convection takes it). Wednesday\nstorm chances are even less certain, but trending downward. \n\nFor Thursday through the next weekend, the GFS and ECMWF ensembles\nare showing a 500mb height anomoly over the Great Lakes (an upper\ntrough) which could cause a round or two of storms in southern WI\nwith the associated frontal passage. That frontal passage could\nmove through southern WI as early as Wednesday night or as late as\nThursday night. While the ECWMF ensemble suggests that the upper \nlow is progressive and quickly brings ridging into southern WI by \nSaturday 8/15, the GFS Ensemble forecasts that this upper low will\nstall over the Northeast through at least the first half of the \nfollowing week.\n\nCronce",
